I have a question concerning different visions of the Lie algebra of an algebraic group. To be more specific, let $G = \operatorname{Spec}(H)$ be a smooth (finitely generated) algebraic group over a field $\mathbb{K}$. We denote by $\epsilon : H \to \mathbb{K}$ the counit. The definition of the Lie algebra of $G$ I have seen goes as follows :
introduce $H^+ := ker(\epsilon)$ and then define:
\begin{equation*}
\mathfrak{g}^* := \frac{H^+}{{H^+}^2}
\end{equation*}
The Lie algebra of $G$ is then defined by:
\begin{equation*}
\mathfrak{g} := Hom_{\mathbb{K}}(\mathfrak{g}^* ,\mathbb{K}) \cong Der_{\epsilon}(H,\mathbb{K})
\end{equation*}
where $Der_{\epsilon}(H,\mathbb{K})$ is the set of derivations relative to the counit, i.e. linear maps $\xi : H \to \mathbb{K}$ such that $\xi (hh') = \xi(h) \epsilon (h') + \epsilon(h) \xi(h')$ for all $h,h' \in H$.
